(window.FREE_Q = window.FREE_Q || {})["xix-b"] = {"qs": [["Taxation", "A Category III AIF trust where each beneficiary's share is clearly determinable is generally taxed:", ["At zero rate", "Only when investors redeem their units, at the rate applicable to each investor", "Only on dividends received", "As a representative assessee on beneficiaries' shares"], 3, "A determinate trust is taxed in a representative capacity, in the same manner as the beneficiaries would be on their shares. Indeterminate trusts may face higher rates."], ["Fees Structure, Fund Performance and Benchmarking", "A fund returned 16% with standard deviation of 12%; the risk-free rate is 7%. Its Sharpe ratio is:", ["0.75", "1.33", "0.58", "0.44"], 0, "Sharpe = (16 − 7) ÷ 12 = 0.75. 1.33 divides the return by volatility without subtracting the risk-free rate."], ["Valuation", "A thinly traded corporate bond with no recent trades is best valued by:", ["Its face value", "The coupon rate", "The issuer's share price", "Using yields of similar bonds with spreads"], 3, "Without trades, fair value can be estimated from yields on comparable bonds adjusted for credit and liquidity differences."], ["Overview of Alternative Investments", "A 'long-short equity' strategy typically aims to:", ["Profit from both rising and falling stocks", "Hold only cash during market downturns", "Track a market index as closely as possible", "Invest only in government bonds"], 0, "Long-short managers buy stocks expected to outperform and short those expected to underperform, so they can earn from stock selection in both directions. Index tracking is a passive approach."], ["Category III AIF: Fund Structures and Service Providers", "In a master-feeder structure, the feeder fund:", ["Trades securities directly while the master only collects fees", "Acts as the custodian of the master fund", "Pools investors and invests into the master", "Calculates NAV for the regulator"], 2, "Feeders collect money from particular investor groups, such as offshore investors, and invest in a master fund that holds the portfolio. Trading happens at master level."], ["Overview of Alternative Investments", "An investor says a fund with high average returns and low volatility is 'obviously safe'. What should an adviser point out?", ["Low volatility always means low risk, so the fund can replace fixed deposits", "Strategies selling options can hide rare large losses", "Average returns guarantee future returns", "Hedge funds cannot have losses above 10%"], 1, "Strategies that sell insurance-like options can earn steady returns until a rare event causes large losses. Volatility does not capture such tail risk."], ["Good Practices", "A distributor recommends switching a client from one Category III fund to another every year without clear reasons. This may indicate:", ["Normal portfolio rebalancing", "Churning for commissions", "Mandatory regulatory action", "Tax-loss harvesting required by law"], 1, "Frequent unexplained switching benefits the distributor more than the client and may be churning. Recommendations must be in the client's interest."], ["Fees Structure, Fund Performance and Benchmarking", "A fund returned 14% with downside deviation of 10%; the risk-free rate is 6%. Its Sortino ratio is:", ["1.40", "0.60", "0.80", "2.33"], 2, "Sortino = (14 − 6) ÷ 10 = 0.80. Unlike Sharpe, it penalises only downside volatility."], ["Growth of AIFs in India and Suitability of Category III AIFs", "A client invests in a Category III fund because last year it returned 45%. The distributor should highlight that:", ["Past returns may not repeat and risks matter", "A 45% return guarantees similar results next year", "SEBI caps losses of Category III funds", "High returns mean the fund is risk-free"], 0, "Past performance is not indicative of future results, especially for leveraged or concentrated strategies. The investor should consider risk, strategy and drawdowns."], ["Good Practices", "Why should a distributor assess a client's entire portfolio before recommending a Category III AIF?", ["To compute the fund's NAV", "To avoid over-concentration in risky products", "To decide the fund's leverage", "To decide the leverage the fund will use for that particular client's units"], 1, "Looking at the full portfolio shows whether the new investment leads to excessive concentration or illiquidity."], ["Category III AIF: Fund Structures and Service Providers", "Units of AIF schemes are required to be held in dematerialised form mainly to:", ["Allow units to be traded on stock exchanges daily", "Improve transparency and record keeping", "Remove the minimum investment requirement", "Avoid KYC"], 1, "Demat holding improves record keeping, transferability and regulatory oversight. It does not make units exchange-traded or waive KYC and minimums."], ["Valuation", "Options held by a fund that are deep out of the money and illiquid should be valued:", ["At the premium originally paid", "At zero always", "At their strike price", "Using a model consistent with market inputs"], 3, "Illiquid options are valued using recognised pricing models with current inputs like volatility. Original premium or strike values do not reflect fair value."], ["Valuation", "A stock held by the fund is suspended from trading. Its valuation should be:", ["The last traded price before suspension, indefinitely and without review", "Zero in all cases", "Fair value determined by an approved policy", "The price at which the fund bought it"], 2, "Suspended securities should be valued at fair value in line with the valuation policy, considering all available information. Mechanically using old prices or zero may misstate NAV."], ["Growth of AIFs in India and Suitability of Category III AIFs", "Which development has supported the growth of Category III AIFs in India?", ["A ban on equity mutual funds for HNIs", "More HNIs seeking differentiated strategies", "Guaranteed returns offered by Category III funds", "Exemption of Category III AIFs from SEBI regulation"], 1, "Growing wealth and demand for strategies beyond long-only funds have driven growth. Category III AIFs are SEBI-regulated and do not guarantee returns."], ["Overview of Alternative Investments", "A 'relative value' strategy seeks to profit from:", ["An overall rise in the stock market", "Long-term holding of real estate", "Mispricing between related securities", "Interest on bank deposits"], 2, "Relative value strategies buy the cheaper and sell the dearer of related instruments, aiming to profit as prices converge, with limited directional exposure."], ["Introduction to Category III AIF Ecosystem", "Which of these is a function of the fund administrator?", ["Marketing the fund to new investors", "Deciding the fund's strategy", "Granting accreditation to investors", "Independent NAV calculation and accounting"], 3, "Administrators calculate NAV, maintain books and reconcile positions independently. Marketing is done by distributors, and accreditation by recognised agencies."], ["Category III AIF Investment Strategies and Due Diligence Process", "A fund holds long positions of ₹120 crore and short positions of ₹80 crore with NAV of ₹100 crore. Its gross and net exposures are:", ["Gross 40%, net 200% long", "Gross 120%, net 80% long", "Gross 200%, net 40% long", "Gross 200%, net 80% short"], 2, "Gross exposure = 120 + 80 = 200 crore (200% of NAV). Net = 120 − 80 = 40 crore long (40% of NAV)."], ["Introduction to Category III AIF Ecosystem", "A Category III fund relies on its prime broker for leverage and stock borrowing. A key risk for investors is:", ["Counterparty risk if the prime broker fails", "Inflation risk on the government bonds the fund holds for margin purposes", "Currency risk on rupee holdings", "Absence of any market risk"], 0, "Heavy reliance on a prime broker exposes the fund to that broker's credit and operational risk; collateral may be at risk if it fails."], ["Growth of AIFs in India and Suitability of Category III AIFs", "A client needs to withdraw her money at short notice for a property purchase next month. A close-ended Category III AIF is:", ["Unsuitable, as money may be locked in", "Ideal, as Category III funds offer daily redemption by law", "Suitable only if the fund is long-only", "Suitable because AIFs guarantee capital"], 0, "Close-ended funds do not allow early redemption, and even open-ended ones may have notice periods. Short-term liquidity needs are best met elsewhere."], ["Fees Structure, Fund Performance and Benchmarking", "A fund's gross return is 18%. It charges a 2% management fee and then 20% of the remaining gain as performance fee. The approximate net return is:", ["14.4%", "12.8%", "16.0%", "12.0%"], 1, "After management fee, return = 16%. Performance fee = 20% × 16% = 3.2%. Net = 16 − 3.2 = 12.8%."], ["Taxation", "Securities Transaction Tax on the fund's trades in listed shares is:", ["Paid directly by each investor", "Refunded by the exchange at year-end", "Borne by the fund as a cost", "Not applicable to AIFs"], 2, "STT applies to trades on recognised exchanges, and the fund pays it as a transaction cost, reducing returns."], ["Fees Structure, Fund Performance and Benchmarking", "NAV rises from ₹100 to ₹125 in a year. There is a 10% hard hurdle and a 20% performance fee. The fee per unit is:", ["₹3", "₹5", "₹2.5", "₹2"], 0, "With a hard hurdle, the fee is only on returns above ₹110: (125 − 110) × 20% = ₹3. With a soft hurdle and full catch-up, the fee would be ₹5."], ["Category III AIF: Fund Structures and Service Providers", "The valuer of a Category III AIF is most important for:", ["Actively traded large-cap shares", "Cash balances", "Illiquid or thinly traded positions", "Exchange-traded futures with daily settlement prices"], 2, "Liquid securities have observable prices; valuers add most value where prices are not readily available and judgement is required."], ["Regulatory Framework", "Category III AIFs may participate in exchange-traded commodity derivatives. This is permitted:", ["Without any limits", "Subject to SEBI's conditions and limits", "Only for agricultural commodities notified by the government", "Only if the fund is close-ended"], 1, "SEBI allows Category III AIFs to trade commodity derivatives within prescribed conditions, including concentration limits per commodity. It is not unlimited."], ["Taxation", "GST on management and performance fees in a Category III AIF is:", ["Paid by SEBI", "Not applicable to fund managers because AIFs are regulated by SEBI", "Charged to the fund and reduces net returns", "Payable only by the distributor"], 2, "Fees are a taxable service, and GST on them is a cost borne by the fund, reducing net returns."], ["Overview of Alternative Investments", "A global macro strategy primarily takes positions based on:", ["Detailed analysis of a single small-cap company", "Views on economies, rates and currencies", "Merger announcements only", "Pairs of closely related stocks"], 1, "Global macro funds trade interest rates, currencies, commodities and indices based on macroeconomic views. Merger arbitrage and pair trades are different strategies."], ["Regulatory Framework", "The minimum corpus requirement for each scheme of a Category III AIF is:", ["₹10 crore", "₹100 crore", "₹20 crore", "₹1 crore"], 2, "Each AIF scheme, including Category III, must have a minimum corpus of ₹20 crore. ₹1 crore is the minimum per-investor investment."], ["Regulatory Framework", "Which body issues accreditation certificates to individuals wishing to be accredited investors?", ["Accreditation agencies recognised by SEBI", "The AIF manager itself", "The Reserve Bank of India", "Any practising chartered accountant who certifies net worth"], 0, "Accreditation is granted by SEBI-recognised accreditation agencies, typically subsidiaries of depositories or exchanges. Managers and CAs cannot accredit investors."], ["Regulatory Framework", "A Category III AIF has NAV of ₹80 crore. Under SEBI's leverage cap, its maximum gross exposure is:", ["₹80 crore", "₹240 crore", "₹120 crore", "₹160 crore"], 3, "Gross exposure of a Category III AIF cannot exceed two times its NAV. 2 × ₹80 crore = ₹160 crore."], ["Regulatory Framework", "A Category III AIF wants to make a material change to its PPM, such as a change in strategy. Investors who disagree should generally be:", ["Bound by the change without recourse", "Charged an exit penalty", "Given an exit option before the change", "Required to double their investment"], 2, "SEBI requires material changes to be communicated with an exit option for dissenting investors, protecting them from unwanted changes."], ["Overview of Alternative Investments", "Which statement about 'absolute return' funds is correct?", ["They guarantee positive returns every year", "They aim for positive returns but do not guarantee them", "They must always outperform the Nifty 50 index in every calendar year", "They are prohibited from using derivatives"], 1, "An absolute-return objective targets positive returns regardless of the market, but it is an aim, not a guarantee. Such funds commonly use derivatives."], ["Introduction to Category III AIF Ecosystem", "Why should the custodian of a Category III AIF be independent of the manager?", ["To allow the manager to trade without records", "To protect assets from misuse by the manager", "Because the custodian decides investments", "Because custodians earn performance fees"], 1, "An independent custodian holds assets separately and reconciles positions, reducing the risk of misappropriation. It does not take investment decisions."], ["Good Practices", "A distributor must keep client information confidential. Sharing it is permitted:", ["With any other fund manager", "With friends who are prospective clients", "With client consent or as required by law", "Whenever the distributor wishes"], 2, "Client data may be shared only with consent or as legally required, such as to regulators. Casual sharing breaches confidentiality."], ["Taxation", "Compared with a PMS, a Category III AIF investor typically:", ["Is not taxed on each underlying trade directly", "Must report every trade in her own return", "Pays no tax at any stage", "Must pay STT separately on each trade"], 0, "In a PMS, the client is taxed on each transaction in her account. In a Category III AIF, tax is mostly paid at the fund level."], ["Category III AIF Investment Strategies and Due Diligence Process", "Why should investors check redemption terms such as notice periods and gates in an open-ended Category III fund?", ["They decide the fund's tax rate", "They are irrelevant for liquid strategies because such funds never restrict exits", "They determine how quickly money can be withdrawn", "They determine the fund's benchmark"], 2, "Notice periods, lock-ins and gates limit redemptions, especially in stress. Investors need to match these with their liquidity needs."], ["Category III AIF: Fund Structures and Service Providers", "A side-by-side structure means that:", ["The AIF has two managers making separate decisions", "Two Indian AIFs merge into one", "An offshore fund invests alongside the Indian AIF in the same deals", "The fund invests side by side with a mutual fund"], 2, "In a side-by-side structure, a parallel offshore fund invests alongside the domestic AIF, typically in the same proportions, under common management."], ["Growth of AIFs in India and Suitability of Category III AIFs", "A client wants a liquid, low-cost diversified equity product with no minimum ticket. Compared with a Category III AIF, the more suitable choice is:", ["A leveraged long-short Category III AIF", "A Category II private equity AIF", "An angel fund", "An equity mutual fund"], 3, "Mutual funds offer liquidity, low minimums and diversification. Category III AIFs require ₹1 crore (except accredited investors) and use complex strategies."], ["Fees Structure, Fund Performance and Benchmarking", "Over the last two years, a hedge-style fund's unit value slid from its high of ₹200 to a low of ₹150 and then recovered. What maximum drawdown should be reported?", ["25%", "33.3%", "50%", "20%"], 0, "Drawdown = (200 − 150) ÷ 200 = 25%. 33.3% divides the fall by the trough instead of the peak."], ["Valuation", "Why should NAV be computed by an independent administrator rather than the manager alone?", ["Because managers are not allowed to see NAV", "Because independent NAV is always higher", "To avoid all audit requirements", "To reduce the risk of manipulating NAV"], 3, "Independent computation provides a check on the manager, whose fees depend on NAV. It does not remove audit requirements."], ["Overview of Alternative Investments", "Compared with traditional investments, alternative investments usually have:", ["Lower fees and daily holdings disclosure", "Higher fees and less transparency", "No minimum investment", "Guaranteed liquidity"], 1, "Alternatives generally carry management and performance fees, limited disclosure and higher minimums. Liquidity is often restricted."], ["Valuation", "A Category III fund has total assets of ₹212 crore, liabilities of ₹12 crore and 1.6 crore units. Its NAV per unit is:", ["₹132.50", "₹125", "₹117.50", "₹140"], 1, "NAV = (212 − 12) ÷ 1.6 = ₹125. ₹132.50 ignores liabilities (212 ÷ 1.6)."], ["Taxation", "When a Category III AIF distributes income on which it has already paid tax, investors are generally:", ["Taxed again at slab rates", "Taxed at 30% flat", "Not taxed again on it", "Liable for GST on it"], 2, "Since tax is paid at fund level, distributions are generally not taxed again in investors' hands, avoiding double taxation."], ["Overview of Alternative Investments", "Which risk is specifically introduced when a fund uses leverage?", ["Magnified losses and margin calls", "Lower expected returns in all market conditions", "Elimination of market risk", "Removal of counterparty risk"], 0, "Leverage enlarges both gains and losses and can force selling when margins are called. It does not remove market or counterparty risk."], ["Category III AIF Investment Strategies and Due Diligence Process", "Which due diligence finding most indicates style drift?", ["A long-only fund holding mostly equities", "An arbitrage fund holding cash-futures positions", "A quant fund using models", "A 'market-neutral' fund running large net long exposure"], 3, "A market-neutral fund should have little net exposure; persistent large net long positions show it has drifted from its stated strategy."], ["Fees Structure, Fund Performance and Benchmarking", "A fund's active return over its benchmark is 4% and its tracking error is 5%. Its information ratio is:", ["1.25", "0.2", "0.8", "9.0"], 2, "Information ratio = active return ÷ tracking error = 4 ÷ 5 = 0.8. It measures consistency of outperformance per unit of active risk."], ["Growth of AIFs in India and Suitability of Category III AIFs", "Accredited investors may invest in Category III AIFs below the usual minimum. Accreditation primarily reflects the investor's:", ["Years of experience with the same bank", "Age above 60", "Income or net worth meeting SEBI's criteria", "Possession of a NISM certificate"], 2, "Accreditation is based on income, net worth or financial assets as set by SEBI, verified by an accreditation agency. Age and certifications are not criteria."], ["Good Practices", "Which statement to a client is appropriate for a long-short fund?", ["The fund cannot lose money because all its positions are fully hedged", "SEBI guarantees the fund's capital", "Returns are fixed at 12% a year", "Hedging reduces some risk but losses are possible"], 3, "Hedged funds can still lose money from stock selection, leverage or basis risk. No return or capital is guaranteed."], ["Introduction to Category III AIF Ecosystem", "The sponsor of a Category III AIF differs from the investment manager in that the sponsor:", ["Makes the daily trading decisions and sets position limits for the portfolio", "Holds custody of securities", "Audits the fund's accounts", "Sets up the AIF and keeps continuing interest"], 3, "The sponsor establishes the fund and maintains the required continuing interest; the manager takes investment decisions. The sponsor and manager may sometimes be the same entity."], ["Introduction to Category III AIF Ecosystem", "A distributor of a Category III AIF should:", ["Promise investors a minimum return", "Manage the fund's portfolio", "Act as the custodian of investor units", "Hold the required NISM certification"], 3, "AIF distributors must hold the relevant NISM certification and assess suitability. They cannot promise returns or perform the manager's or custodian's functions."], ["Regulatory Framework", "While computing leverage for a Category III AIF, positions taken purely for hedging that meet SEBI's conditions:", ["Must be doubled in exposure computation", "May be offset in computing exposure", "Are always prohibited", "Are ignored only for open-ended funds"], 1, "SEBI's methodology allows offsetting of eligible hedging and portfolio-rebalancing positions when calculating exposure, so genuine hedges do not consume the leverage limit."], ["Good Practices", "While pitching a leveraged Category III fund, a distributor shows the client only its best annual returns and does not mention its past drawdowns. This conduct is:", ["Acceptable marketing practice", "Required by SEBI", "Incomplete disclosure amounting to mis-selling", "Only a minor lapse if returns are high"], 2, "Investors must be told about risks, including leverage and drawdowns. Highlighting only returns is misleading."], ["Category III AIF Investment Strategies and Due Diligence Process", "Which question is most relevant when assessing a manager's risk management framework?", ["Who sets and monitors risk limits independently?", "How many branches does the manager have?", "What is the manager's advertising budget?", "Does the manager sponsor sports events?"], 0, "Independent setting and monitoring of position, leverage and loss limits is central to risk management. Marketing activities do not indicate risk discipline."], ["Growth of AIFs in India and Suitability of Category III AIFs", "An investor's total investable portfolio is ₹20 crore and she has a high risk appetite. Investing ₹2 crore in a Category III AIF would most likely be viewed as:", ["Unsuitable, because she exceeds the minimum ticket", "Prohibited, as AIF investments must exceed 50% of wealth", "Unsuitable, since high-risk investors cannot invest in AIFs", "A reasonable satellite allocation"], 3, "A 10% allocation for an experienced, high-risk investor with ample wealth is a reasonable satellite position. There is no rule requiring a minimum share of wealth."], ["Category III AIF Investment Strategies and Due Diligence Process", "A fund holds long stocks of ₹100 crore with beta 1.2 and short index futures of ₹80 crore (beta 1.0). Its beta-adjusted net exposure is about:", ["₹20 crore long", "₹40 crore long", "₹180 crore long", "₹40 crore short"], 1, "Beta-adjusted long = 100 × 1.2 = 120; short = 80 × 1.0 = 80. Net = 120 − 80 = ₹40 crore long."], ["Growth of AIFs in India and Suitability of Category III AIFs", "Compared with a PMS, a key difference of a Category III AIF is that:", ["Investors hold units of a pooled fund", "Investors hold securities directly in their own demat accounts", "There is no minimum investment", "It cannot use derivatives"], 0, "In an AIF, investors own units of a pooled vehicle, while in a PMS the securities are held in the client's own name. Category III AIFs can use derivatives."], ["Introduction to Category III AIF Ecosystem", "Which party in a Category III AIF is responsible for independently auditing the fund's financial statements?", ["Statutory auditor", "Prime broker", "Investment manager", "Distributor"], 0, "The statutory auditor gives an independent opinion on the fund's accounts. The prime broker provides financing and trading services, and the manager runs the portfolio."], ["Taxation", "Why might a Category III fund's high tax rate at the trust level matter to a low-income investor?", ["She gets a refund of all tax paid by the fund", "She cannot use her lower slab on fund income", "Her income becomes exempt", "She pays tax twice on every rupee"], 1, "Because tax is paid at fund level, often at the maximum marginal rate, a low-bracket investor cannot benefit from her lower personal rate."], ["Category III AIF: Fund Structures and Service Providers", "Which service provider is most critical for executing a high-frequency quantitative strategy?", ["A real estate valuer", "A legal adviser on property titles", "An insurance surveyor", "A broker with robust execution technology"], 3, "Quantitative strategies depend on reliable, low-latency execution and connectivity from brokers. Property-related service providers are irrelevant."], ["Valuation", "Overvaluing illiquid holdings in an open-ended fund harms:", ["Remaining investors, as redeemers exit at inflated NAV", "Only the manager, as its fees fall whenever the portfolio is overvalued", "No one, as values eventually adjust", "Only new investors who buy at low NAV"], 0, "Redeeming investors are paid more than the true value, leaving remaining investors with the shortfall. Overvaluation also inflates fees."], ["Regulatory Framework", "Which is an obligation of a Category III AIF manager under SEBI's framework?", ["Disclosing NAV to investors at prescribed frequency", "Guaranteeing a minimum return to investors", "Distributing all realised profits to investors every month as dividends", "Listing the units on a stock exchange"], 0, "Managers must compute and disclose NAV periodically as prescribed. Guaranteeing returns or monthly payouts is not required, and listing is not mandatory."], ["Category III AIF: Fund Structures and Service Providers", "An open-ended Category III AIF differs from a close-ended one in that it:", ["Allows periodic subscriptions and redemptions", "Can never use leverage", "Has a fixed maturity date of three years that cannot be extended by investors", "Is exempt from SEBI regulation"], 0, "Open-ended funds accept subscriptions and allow redemptions at specified intervals, often with notice periods. Category III funds of either type may use leverage within limits."], ["Category III AIF Investment Strategies and Due Diligence Process", "A statistical arbitrage strategy typically:", ["Takes large concentrated bets on single companies", "Invests in real estate projects", "Holds stocks for many years without trading", "Trades many small pricing anomalies using models"], 3, "Statistical arbitrage uses quantitative models to exploit short-term mispricings across many securities, relying on diversification and execution efficiency."], ["Fees Structure, Fund Performance and Benchmarking", "Why should an investor care about how often a Category III fund crystallises its performance fee?", ["It decides the leverage limit SEBI allows the fund to use in a given year", "More frequent crystallisation can raise total fees", "It sets the minimum investment", "It has no effect on investor returns"], 1, "If fees crystallise often, managers may earn fees on interim gains that later reverse, raising total fees in volatile periods. Annual crystallisation is generally more investor-friendly."], ["Overview of Alternative Investments", "Why might the historical correlation of a hedge-fund strategy with equities rise during a market crisis?", ["Liquidity stress makes many assets fall together", "Hedge funds are required by law to buy equities in a crisis", "Short positions automatically become long positions", "Correlations are fixed and cannot change"], 0, "In crises, deleveraging and liquidity shortages lead to simultaneous selling, raising correlations precisely when diversification is most needed."], ["Valuation", "Accrued but unpaid management fees should be treated in NAV computation as:", ["An asset that increases NAV", "Ignored until paid", "Part of investors' capital", "A liability that reduces NAV"], 3, "Accrued fees are obligations of the fund and must be deducted as liabilities to present the true NAV."], ["Category III AIF: Fund Structures and Service Providers", "A Category III fund issues a separate unit class with a lower management fee for investors committing more than ₹25 crore. This is permissible if:", ["It is disclosed and within SEBI's rules", "It is kept confidential from smaller investors", "The sponsor guarantees returns to that class", "The class is not reported to SEBI"], 0, "Differential fee classes are allowed when disclosed and consistent with SEBI's framework on investor rights. Secrecy or guarantees are not permitted."], ["Category III AIF: Fund Structures and Service Providers", "Why is a trust structure common for Category III AIFs in India?", ["Trusts pay no tax on any income they earn, whatever its nature or source", "It is flexible and familiar to Indian investors", "Only trusts can use derivatives", "Trusts are not regulated by SEBI"], 1, "Trusts are simple to establish and wind up and are widely used. They are taxed under trust-taxation rules, can be used for any category, and remain SEBI-regulated."], ["Regulatory Framework", "A Category III AIF has a corpus of ₹300 crore. The minimum continuing interest of the sponsor or manager is:", ["₹15 crore", "₹7.5 crore", "₹5 crore", "₹10 crore"], 3, "For Category III, continuing interest is the lower of 5% of corpus or ₹10 crore. 5% of ₹300 crore is ₹15 crore, so the requirement is ₹10 crore."], ["Valuation", "Exchange-traded futures held by a Category III AIF are usually valued at:", ["The exchange's daily settlement price", "The price at which the contract was opened", "The underlying stock's 52-week high", "The strike price of related options"], 0, "Futures are marked to the exchange settlement price each day, which reflects current market value."], ["Taxation", "A Category III fund frequently trades listed shares within days. Its gains are likely to be characterised as:", ["Long-term capital gains", "Exempt income", "Income from house property", "Business income"], 3, "High-frequency trading indicates a trading business, so gains may be treated as business income. Long-term capital gains need longer holding periods."], ["Category III AIF Investment Strategies and Due Diligence Process", "In merger arbitrage, the main risk is that:", ["Interest rates on bank deposits fall", "The acquirer pays a dividend", "The deal fails and the spread widens", "Index futures expire"], 2, "Merger arbitrage profits from the spread between the target's price and the deal value; if the deal fails, the target's price usually falls sharply."], ["Category III AIF Investment Strategies and Due Diligence Process", "An options-selling volatility strategy shows steady small gains for years. The main due diligence concern is:", ["Too much diversification", "Lack of any premium income", "Exposure to rare but severe losses", "High dividend payouts"], 2, "Selling options earns premiums regularly but can produce large losses during sharp market moves. Due diligence should test stress scenarios and risk limits."], ["Taxation", "A Category III fund earns income from derivatives traded on recognised exchanges. Such income is generally treated as:", ["Speculative income", "Non-speculative business income", "Exempt income", "Capital gains in all cases"], 1, "Eligible derivative transactions on recognised exchanges are excluded from speculative transactions, so income is generally non-speculative business income."], ["Fees Structure, Fund Performance and Benchmarking", "A unit's NAV is ₹125 at the year-end. The high-water mark is ₹115. With a 20% performance fee and no hurdle, the fee per unit is:", ["₹5", "₹2", "₹3", "₹25"], 1, "Fee applies only to gains above the high-water mark: (125 − 115) × 20% = ₹2. ₹5 would apply the fee to gains from ₹100."], ["Good Practices", "How may a Category III AIF pay commission to the distributors who place its units with investors?", ["Fully upfront at the time of investment", "Only as a one-time fee on exit", "Only on an equal-trail basis", "As a share of the manager's performance fee"], 2, "SEBI requires Category III distribution fees to be paid only as equal trail, with no upfront payment, to discourage churning."], ["Good Practices", "A distributor receives complaints from a client about delayed redemption. The distributor should:", ["Ignore it, as it concerns the manager", "Ignore it, as redemption issues concern only the manager and not the distributor", "Withdraw the client's units personally", "Help escalate it to the manager and SEBI channels"], 3, "Distributors should help clients raise grievances with the manager and, if needed, through SEBI's SCORES and ODR mechanisms."], ["Introduction to Category III AIF Ecosystem", "Which service provider would most likely detect a mismatch between the manager's reported positions and actual holdings?", ["The fund's marketing agency", "The investor relations team of the manager, while preparing quarterly letters", "The distributor", "The administrator reconciling with custodian records"], 3, "Independent administrators reconcile manager records with custodian and broker statements, flagging discrepancies. Marketing and distribution functions do not do this."], ["Growth of AIFs in India and Suitability of Category III AIFs", "Which factor is MOST important in assessing a client's suitability for a Category III AIF?", ["The client's preferred bank branch", "Understanding of complex strategies and ability to bear losses", "Whether the client already holds a credit card issued by the sponsor's bank", "The client's city of residence"], 1, "Category III AIFs may use leverage, derivatives and short selling; investors must understand these and be able to absorb losses. Banking preferences are irrelevant."], ["Introduction to Category III AIF Ecosystem", "The registrar and transfer agent of a Category III AIF mainly handles:", ["Trading decisions and risk limits", "Securities lending to the fund and financing of its leveraged positions", "Selection of stocks", "Investor records and unit transactions"], 3, "The RTA maintains investor records, processes subscriptions and redemptions and services investors. Trading decisions rest with the manager."], ["Good Practices", "A client's risk profile shows low risk tolerance, but he insists on a high-risk Category III AIF. A good distributor should:", ["Document the mismatch and explain the risks", "Hide the risk profile result", "Change the risk profile to match the product", "Promise to cover losses"], 0, "The distributor should explain the mismatch, record the client's informed decision and not alter the profile. Promising to cover losses is prohibited."]]};
